# Freeze the character tools/keys/hunger-penalty.keys builds, so every check that
# runs that script can load him instead of rolling him again.
#
#   INCURSION_OPTIONS=tools/fixtures/options-2026-08-22.dat \
#       tools/headless.sh tools/keys/hunger-penalty-save.keys 1
#
# THE CHARGEN BELOW IS A COPY of tools/keys/hunger-penalty.keys lines 1-65,
# deliberately unchanged, and the original is NOT edited. That script builds its
# character inline rather than including a shared chargen-*.keys, so there is
# nothing to @include. Copying rather than refactoring means no existing check
# changes what it measures. The same choice, and the same warning, are in
# tools/keys/lizardfolk-monk-save.keys: do not "improve" this copy -- the
# fixture is only worth having if it holds THE character that script rolls.
#
# WHY A CHARACTER IS FROZEN AT ALL: see the header of
# tools/keys/chargen-default-save.keys. bd inc-vrt8.

# Make a Dragonkin, let him grow Hungry, photograph him, let him grow
# Starving, photograph him again.
#
# ONE ASSERTION, bd inc-i9q.3: getting hungrier must never make a character
# stronger. The sidebar prints STR and CON (src/Term.cpp), and those two
# numbers must not go UP between the two photographs.
#
# WHY A DRAGONKIN. The ordinary once-per-turn hunger tick does nothing at all
# for anybody -- that is bd inc-i9q.2, a separate defect, still open. A
# Dragonkin has Burning Hunger, which calls Creature::GetHungrier with 6
# rather than 1 and so takes the arm of that function which works. He is
# therefore the only character in the game who can be made hungry in a
# scripted session of any reasonable length. When inc-i9q.2 is fixed this
# script will still work; it will simply arrive sooner.
#
# THE COUNTS. Each Search command spends about 100 points of hunger, from a
# starting 2490. Hungry runs from 1000 down to 481 and Starving from 480 down
# to 241 (inc/Defines.h:3504-3512), so eleven Searches land inside Hungry and
# four more land inside Starving. The sidebar prints the state as a word, and
# tools/check_hunger_penalty.sh refuses to compare two photographs that are
# not one Hungry and one Starving.
#
# Race, subrace, class and alignment use @choose by name, so changing a
# list cannot shift these selections. Run with a fixed seed;
# tools/check_hunger_penalty.sh supplies one. Attribute sets have no names,
# and the rolls decide which feats are offered, so those picks stay counted.
# Start-menu, yes/no and movement keys stay positional for the reasons below.

@expect "Initial Choices"
a       # Create a New Character: start menu is not a screen @choose reads.
@expect "Alter the character generation options?"
n       # No alteration: a yes/no question, not a menu.
@expect "Explore Mode. Continue?"
y       # Continue in Explore Mode: a yes/no question, not a menu.
@choose "Lizardfolk"
@choose "Dragonkin"
@choose "Barbarian"
@expect "Which of the five attribute sets"
a       # First attribute set: the five offered sets have no names.
@choose "Neutral"
@expect "Choose a feat"
a       # First feat: the offered feats depend on attribute rolls.
@expect "Choose a feat"
a       # Second feat: the offered feats depend on attribute rolls.

# Skill Manager. Three RIGHTs per skill spends everything available
# without needing to know how many ranks this character has.
# The old comment said Skill Manager twice; corrected 2026-09-12.
@expect "Skill Manager"
# Cursor movement, not a choice.
RIGHT*3 DOWN RIGHT*3 DOWN RIGHT*3 DOWN RIGHT*3 DOWN RIGHT*3 DOWN RIGHT*3 DOWN
RIGHT*3 DOWN RIGHT*3 DOWN RIGHT*3 DOWN RIGHT*3 DOWN RIGHT*3 DOWN RIGHT*3 DOWN
# Finish ranks, then confirm: ENTER is a control; y answers yes/no.
ENTER y

@expect "Your personality archetype"
y       # No match: the sixteen personality entries use [a] through [p].
# The old comment called this worship; corrected 2026-09-12.
# RIGHT/DOWN move the personality selection to The Manipulative Plotter.
# Personality affects only the sheet banner, so its selection is not pinned.

RIGHT*3 DOWN RIGHT*3 DOWN RIGHT*3 DOWN RIGHT*3 DOWN RIGHT*3 DOWN RIGHT*3 DOWN
RIGHT*3 DOWN RIGHT*3 DOWN RIGHT*3 DOWN RIGHT*3 DOWN RIGHT*3 DOWN RIGHT*3 DOWN
# Accept the personality, then dismiss the Entry Chamber description.
ENTER y

@dump:arrival

# HOW MANY KEYS THE OPENING WANTS IS A PROPERTY OF THE CHARACTER, so no fixed
# count is right for every chargen. Measured 2026-09-17: tools/keys/chargen.keys
# leaves NOTHING pending and reaches the System Menu on the first ESC, while
# tools/keys/chargen-rogue.keys leaves a god's speech and an AutoHide line, so
# the first ESC only dismisses a message. A hard-coded ESC ENTER SPACE, copied
# from tools/keys/lizardfolk-monk-save.keys, failed in both directions: it
# walked INTO the menu for the barbarian and fell short of it for the rogue.
# @until sends ESC as many times as this character needs and stops the moment
# the menu is up (src/Wposix.cpp:1318-1326).
# CHARGEN IS NOT OVER UNTIL THE ARCHETYPE IS PICKED, and ESC will not dismiss
# that screen -- it is a menu wanting a letter. [a] is The Ambitious Conqueror;
# the choice only has to be the SAME one every time. Harmless where the copied
# chargen above already picked one: @while sends nothing if the screen is gone.
@while "personality archetype" a

@until "System Menu" ESC

# @until only warns when it gives up (src/Wposix.cpp:1568-1571), so the
# assertion stays: it stops the run at exit 6 rather than pressing a letter
# into whatever screen is really there. [a] beside [b] is Save and Quit.
@expect "System Menu"
b                       # [b] Save and Continue (src/Player.cpp:843)

# The save is not written by the keypress. Case 2 of the System Menu sets the
# autosave flag and Timeout = 1 (src/Player.cpp:851-853); Game::Play writes the
# file at the top of the next pass of its loop (src/Main.cpp:266-275). This
# dump therefore fires AFTER the file is on disk, and photographs the engine's
# own "Autosave... done." line saying so.
@dump:saved

# NO @quit HERE, DELIBERATELY. @quit becomes KY_CMD_QUICK_QUIT
# (src/Wposix.cpp:1657-1664), which is Save and Quit: it would write the save a
# SECOND time and shuffle the first copy off to <name>.sav.backup, leaving the
# fixture a file no line of this script asked for. Letting the key script run
# out instead ends the session with exactly one save.
